UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047759921?origin=resultslist
AB  - Federated Learning (FL) shifts machine learning from centralized data aggregation to distributed training on local devices or servers, preserving privacy by keeping raw data decentralized. Federated governance emerges as a hybrid data governance model tailored to FL, blending centralized policy enforcement with local team autonomy to enable secure, compliant model training. This chapter studies the use of federated governance, a hybrid approach that strikes a compromise between local domain team’s autonomous execution and centralized policy-setting. It focuses at important elements like: Governed FL Frameworks: The incorporation of technological connections (such as Eclipse Dataspace Components) to enforce use guidelines and data sovereignty when training models in delicate industries like healthcare. Organizational Archetypes: Mapping organizational preparedness according to data ownership, infrastructure, and market position using decision frameworks. Data Quality Governance: Mechanisms to guarantee that heterogeneous, non-IId (non-independent and identically distributed) data across several participants satisfies quality requirements to avoid global model deterioration are known as data quality governance. Policy-as-Code: The transition to automated, codified governance in which training pipelines directly incorporate rules for data access, masking (such as PII protection), and compliance. Security and Trust: Secure aggregation and differential privacy increase security and trust by reducing risks including model inversion, poisoning, and data reconstruction. The transition toward Data Mesh architectures in 2025, where domain teams control data products, is highlighted in the chapter’s conclusion. This will change governance from static compliance to a strategic facilitator for collaborative AI. © 2027 Suvarna Sharma, Jeetendra Kumar, Rashmi Gupta, Valentina E. Balas and Gaurav Dhiman.
