AB  - This article addresses the challenge of information support for digital twins in materials science, specifically focusing on the heterogeneity, large volumes, and unstructured nature of experimental and computational data. A comprehensive Data Lake architecture based on the Apache Hadoop ecosystem is proposed, integrating hybrid optimization algorithms for multi-criteria searching of material properties. The scientific novelty lies in developing a method to transform semi-structured output files from quantum chemical calculations (CRYSTAL) into standardized electronic passports in JSON format, followed by distributed storage and indexing in HBase. The theoretical contribution is validated by estimating the algorithmic complexity of search procedures, demonstrating a reduction from linear to logarithmic time. The practical value is confirmed through experimental performance comparisons and the official registration of a software suite that automates data processing and enables rapid searching for materials with desired properties, such as band gap energy, thermodynamic parameters, and optical characteristics. The proposed architecture creates a scalable foundation for applying machine learning and advanced optimization in the development of new materials.  © 2026 IEEE.
